from qlib import Q
C='ownership-estates'
Q(C,'basic',"The highest and most complete form of ownership in real property is:","Fee simple absolute",[("Life estate","A life estate lasts only for a person's lifetime."),("Fee simple determinable","A determinable fee can end automatically if a condition is broken."),("Estate for years","An estate for years is a lease, not ownership.")],"Fee simple absolute = most complete ownership","**Fee simple absolute** gives the owner the full **bundle of rights**: to possess, use, enjoy, exclude others, and dispose of the property. It has no time limit and can be inherited.",5,["estates"])
Q(C,'basic',"The \"bundle of rights\" of an owner includes all of the following rights EXCEPT the right to:","Use the property in violation of zoning laws",[("Possess the property","Possession is one of the rights in the bundle."),("Exclude others from the property","The right to exclude is part of the bundle."),("Sell or give away the property","The right of disposition is part of the bundle.")],"Bundle of rights is limited by government powers","The **bundle of rights** includes possession, control, enjoyment, exclusion, and disposition. These rights are always subject to government powers such as police power, so an owner has no right to break zoning laws.",4,["bundle of rights"],lock=True)
Q(C,'intermediate',"Grandmother deeds her house to her son \"for the life of my daughter.\" The son holds a:","Life estate pur autre vie",[("Fee simple absolute","The son's interest ends when the daughter dies, so it is not fee simple."),("Remainder interest","A remainder is the future interest of the person who receives the property after a life estate."),("Leasehold estate","A leasehold is a tenant's right under a lease.")],"Life estate measured by another's life = pur autre vie","A **life estate pur autre vie** lasts for the life of someone **other** than the life tenant. Here the son owns it, but it ends when the daughter dies.",3,["life estate"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"An owner gives a life estate to her brother and says that after his death the property goes to her niece. The niece's interest is a:","Remainder",[("Reversion","A reversion is when the property returns to the original grantor or her heirs."),("Leasehold","A leasehold is a tenant's right to possess under a lease."),("Easement","An easement is a right to use land, not a future ownership interest.")],"Third party after life estate = remainderman","When a life estate ends, the property goes either back to the grantor (**reversion**) or to a named third person (**remainder**). The niece is the **remainderman**.",4,["life estate"])
Q(C,'basic',"Two co-owners hold title with the right of survivorship. They own as:","Joint tenants",[("Tenants in common","Tenants in common have no right of survivorship; each share passes to heirs."),("Owners in severalty","Severalty means one owner alone."),("Life tenants","A life tenant holds a life estate, not co-ownership with survivorship.")],"Joint tenancy = right of survivorship","**Joint tenancy** includes the **right of survivorship**: when one joint tenant dies, that share goes to the surviving joint tenants, not to the deceased owner's heirs.",5,["co-ownership"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Which four \"unities\" are required to create a joint tenancy?","Time, title, interest, and possession",[("Time, money, interest, and survivorship","Money and survivorship are not among the four unities."),("Title, consent, possession, and recording","Consent and recording are not among the four unities."),("Interest, marriage, title, and time","Marriage is not required for joint tenancy.")],"Joint tenancy needs T-T-I-P","A joint tenancy needs the four unities, remembered as **T-T-I-P**: same **time** of acquiring, same **title** (deed), equal **interest**, and equal right of **possession**.",4,["co-ownership"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A, B, and C own land as joint tenants. C sells her interest to D. What is the result?","A and B are joint tenants with each other, and D is a tenant in common with them",[("A, B, and D are all joint tenants","D received title at a different time and by a different deed, so D cannot be a joint tenant."),("The whole joint tenancy ends and all become tenants in common","Only C's share leaves the joint tenancy; A and B remain joint tenants between themselves."),("The sale is void because joint tenants cannot sell","A joint tenant may sell her interest; doing so breaks the joint tenancy for that share.")],"Sale by one joint tenant → buyer is a tenant in common","When one joint tenant sells, the buyer does not share the unities of time and title, so the buyer becomes a **tenant in common**. The remaining owners stay **joint tenants** with each other. D owns 1/3 as a tenant in common.",4,["co-ownership"])
Q(C,'basic',"In a condominium, each owner holds title to the unit and also:","An undivided interest in the common elements",[("Shares of stock in a corporation that owns the building","Stock ownership with a proprietary lease describes a cooperative."),("A leasehold in the land under the building","Condominium owners usually own, not lease, their share of the land."),("Only the right to use the unit for one week a year","That describes a timeshare.")],"Condo = own unit + share of common elements","In a **condominium**, each owner holds fee title to a unit plus an **undivided interest in the common elements** (halls, roof, grounds). In a **cooperative**, the residents own shares in a corporation and lease their units.",4,["common-interest ownership"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Which lien is generally paid first, ahead of even an earlier recorded mortgage?","A real property tax lien",[("A second mortgage","A second mortgage is junior to the first mortgage."),("A judgment lien","Judgment liens are usually ranked by the date they attach."),("A mechanic's lien","Mechanic's lien priority depends on state rules, but tax liens come first.")],"Property tax liens have top priority","Most liens are ranked by the date they were recorded (\"first in time, first in right\"). **Real property tax and special assessment liens** are the main exception: they usually come first, ahead of mortgages recorded earlier.",4,["liens"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A lien that attaches to all of a debtor's real property in the county, such as a court judgment, is called a:","General lien",[("Specific lien","A specific lien attaches only to one property, such as a mortgage."),("Voluntary lien","A judgment is involuntary; the debtor did not agree to it."),("Mechanic's lien","A mechanic's lien is a specific lien on the property that was improved.")],"General lien = all of debtor's property","A **general lien** affects all property of the debtor, such as a **judgment lien** or a federal income tax lien. A **specific lien** affects one property, such as a mortgage, a property tax lien, or a mechanic's lien.",3,["liens"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A tenant stays after the lease has expired without the landlord's consent. The tenant now has a:","Tenancy at sufferance",[("Periodic tenancy","A periodic tenancy renews automatically, such as month to month, with the landlord's consent."),("Estate for years","An estate for years has a definite end date that has already passed here."),("Tenancy at will","A tenancy at will exists with the landlord's consent for no fixed term.")],"Holdover without consent = tenancy at sufferance","A **tenancy at sufferance** is the weakest leasehold: a tenant who once had a lawful right stays after it ends **without the landlord's consent**. If the landlord accepts rent, it may turn into a periodic tenancy under state law.",4,["leasehold estates"])
C='deeds-title-recording'
Q(C,'basic',"Which deed gives the buyer the greatest protection?","General warranty deed",[("Quitclaim deed","A quitclaim deed makes no warranties at all."),("Special warranty deed","A special warranty deed covers only the time the seller owned the property."),("Bargain and sale deed","A bargain and sale deed implies the seller holds title but makes few or no warranties.")],"Most protection = general warranty deed","A **general warranty deed** has the most covenants (promises), such as seisin, quiet enjoyment, against encumbrances, and warranty forever. The seller defends title against claims from **any time** in the past.",5,["deeds"])
Q(C,'basic',"A deed that transfers whatever interest the grantor may have, with no warranties, is a:","Quitclaim deed",[("General warranty deed","A general warranty deed has the most warranties."),("Special warranty deed","A special warranty deed warrants title during the seller's ownership."),("Trustee's deed","A trustee's deed is used by a trustee and normally recites the trustee's authority.")],"No warranties = quitclaim deed","A **quitclaim deed** gives up any interest the grantor might have, without promising that the grantor has any. It is often used to clear a **cloud on title** or to transfer between family members.",5,["deeds"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Which of the following is required for a deed to be valid?","The signature of the grantor",[("The signature of the grantee","Usually only the grantor signs the deed."),("Recording in the public records","Recording protects the buyer but is not required for the deed to be valid between the parties."),("A statement of the exact purchase price","A deed needs consideration but not the exact price.")],"Valid deed: grantor signs, delivery and acceptance","A valid deed must be in writing, name a competent **grantor** and an identifiable grantee, have a legal description, words of conveyance, consideration, the **grantor's signature**, and be **delivered and accepted**. Recording is not needed for validity but gives public notice.",5,["deeds"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Title to real property generally passes from the seller to the buyer when the deed is:","Delivered by the grantor and accepted by the grantee",[("Signed by the grantor","Signing alone does not transfer title; the deed must be delivered."),("Recorded","Recording gives notice but title passes on delivery and acceptance."),("Notarized","An acknowledgment allows recording but does not itself transfer title.")],"Title passes on delivery and acceptance","A deed does not transfer title until it is **delivered** by the grantor (with the intent to transfer) and **accepted** by the grantee. Acknowledgment before a notary lets the deed be recorded.",4,["deeds"])
Q(C,'basic',"Recording a deed in the public records gives:","Constructive notice to everyone",[("Actual notice to everyone","Actual notice is knowledge a person really has, such as from seeing something."),("A guarantee that the title is free of defects","Recording does not guarantee title; title insurance protects against defects."),("Validity to an otherwise invalid deed","Recording cannot make an invalid deed valid.")],"Recording = constructive notice","**Constructive notice** means the law treats everyone as knowing what is in the public records, whether they looked or not. **Actual notice** is what a person really knows, such as seeing someone living in the property.",5,["recording"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"The history of all recorded documents affecting a property's title, in order, is called the:","Chain of title",[("Title insurance policy","A title policy insures against defects; it is not the history itself."),("Certificate of occupancy","A certificate of occupancy states a building is fit to occupy."),("Plat map","A plat map shows lots and blocks in a subdivision.")],"Chain of title = sequence of owners; abstract = summary","The **chain of title** is the sequence of ownership from the original source to the present owner. An **abstract of title** is a summary of the recorded documents. A gap in the chain may need a **quiet title** action.",4,["title"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Which of the following protects a buyer against a forged deed found in the chain of title after closing?","An owner's title insurance policy",[("A recorded quitclaim deed from the buyer","The buyer quitclaiming would give away the buyer's own interest."),("The lender's title insurance policy","A lender's policy protects only the lender up to the loan balance."),("An attorney's opinion of title","An attorney's opinion can be wrong and does not pay claims like insurance does.")],"Owner's policy protects the buyer; lender's policy protects the lender","**Title insurance** pays for covered losses from title defects, including hidden risks like **forgery** that a search may not find. An **owner's policy** protects the buyer; a **lender's (mortgagee's) policy** protects only the lender.",4,["title insurance"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Gaining title to another person's land by open, notorious, hostile, and continuous possession for a period set by state law is called:","Adverse possession",[("Escheat","Escheat gives property to the state when there are no heirs."),("Eminent domain","Eminent domain is a government taking for public use."),("Devise","A devise is a gift of real property by will.")],"Adverse possession = involuntary alienation by long open use","**Adverse possession** is a form of **involuntary alienation**. The possessor must use the land openly, notoriously, hostilely (without permission), exclusively, and continuously for the time set by state law. A quiet title suit often confirms the new title.",4,["involuntary alienation"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A transfer of real property by will is called a:","Devise",[("Bequest","A bequest is a gift of personal property by will."),("Escheat","Escheat is a transfer to the state when there is no will and no heirs."),("Intestate succession","Intestate succession is transfer when there is no will.")],"Real property by will = devise; person receiving = devisee","Transferring **real property by will** is a **devise**, and the person who receives it is the **devisee**. When someone dies without a will, the property passes by **intestate succession** under state law.",3,["voluntary alienation"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A buyer finds that a seller's late uncle may have had an interest in the property that was never released. This is a:","Cloud on title, which may be cleared by a quitclaim deed or quiet title action",[("Constructive notice, which has no effect on title","An unreleased interest is a defect that must be cleared."),("Easement appurtenant that runs with the land","Nothing suggests an easement."),("Lis pendens that the buyer can ignore","A lis pendens is a notice of a pending lawsuit; it cannot be ignored either.")],"Cloud on title → quitclaim or quiet title suit","A **cloud on title** is any claim or document that may weaken the owner's title. It can often be removed with a **quitclaim deed** from the person who may have the interest, or by a court **quiet title** action.",4,["title"])
